   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to a provisional permit for\nclinical laboratory technology; and to repeal subdivision 2 of section 8610 of\nthe education law relating to a limited license for clinical laboratory\ntechnology",
    "basePrintNo": "S8140",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8140-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§8608 & 8610, rpld §8610 sub 2, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8140\n\nSPONSOR: DEFRANCISCO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to a\nprovisional permit for clinical laboratory technology; and to repeal\nsubdivision 2 of section 8610 of the education law relating to a limited\nlicense for clinical laboratory technology\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS OF BILL:\n\nSection 1: Amends Section 8608 Education Law, which would create a\nprovisional permit for clinical laboratory personnel and sets out the\nrequirements to qualify for the permit.\n\nSection 2: Repeals Section 8610 Subdivision 2 of the Education Law.\nThis repeals the previous limited licensing system that was set to\nexpire on 9/1/18.\n\nSection 3: Establishes the Effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThere is an annual demand of approximately 640 new laboratory profes-\nsionals while only approximately 230 are graduated annually. In addi-\ntion, it has been estimated that 10% of the current workforce plans to\nretire within the next five years.\n\nThis bill would replace the current limited license provision for clin-\nical laboratory personnel, which sunsets every few years, and replace\nthe limited license with a provisional permit. The limited license has\nalways been a short term fix to the laboratory staffing problem, and the\naddition of a provisional permit will serve as a long term solution that\nwill allow laboratories to recruit quality personnel that need time to\ncomplete certain educational or testing requirements that are unique to\nNew York.\n\nThis proposal would create a new provisional permit that will allow an\napplicant to work, under supervision, pending the completion of work\nspecific to New York rules and regulations. The permit would be good for\none year and could be renewed for an additional year to allow for\ncompletion of all licensing deficiencies. The education requirements for\nbeing granted a provisional permit are the same requirements that were\napproved and used by the State Education Department for the issuance of\nthe Limited License.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNominal\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
